  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (D): Per ASC 255-10-05 and macroeconomic theory, demand-pull inflation occurs when aggregate demand exceeds aggregate supply, often driven by excessive money growth or consumer optimism. Cost-push inflation results from rising input costs such as wages and raw materials that shift the aggregate supply curve leftward.
Wrong (A): Both types of inflation can arise from either monetary or fiscal causes; the distinction is demand-side versus supply-side origin, not the policy tool involved.
Wrong (B): Both demand-pull and cost-push inflation can occur during any phase of the business cycle; cost-push inflation during stagnant output is known as stagflation.
