  explanation = $EXPL$Correct (A): Per ASC 825-10-50 and Federal Reserve open market operations, purchasing Treasury securities increases bank reserves and the money supply by crediting the reserve accounts of selling banks. The increased supply of reserves puts downward pressure on short-term interest rates, making this the primary mechanism of expansionary monetary policy.
Wrong (B): Purchasing securities has the opposite effect of what this choice describes; it increases the money supply and lowers rates rather than decreasing the supply and raising rates.
Wrong (C): Open market purchases affect both the money supply and interest rates simultaneously; the federal funds rate moves as a consequence of changed reserve levels.
